<BaseLayout title="Mary Galley research | Medway Genealogy" description="Evidence for identifying Mary Galley, wife of Matthew Medway, with the Lytchett Minster baptism of 1760.">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">Research case</div>
    <h1>Mary Galley: the Lytchett–Hamworthy hypothesis</h1>
    <p class="lead">The leading candidate for the Mary Galley who married Matthew Medway at Poole St James in 1786 is Mary, daughter of William Galley, baptised at Lytchett Minster on 20 April 1760. The identification is not yet proved, but recent lease, militia and collateral-family evidence has made the case substantially stronger.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Current assessment:</strong> strong leading candidate, not proved. The principal contrary evidence is Mary Medway's reported age of 88 at her 1842 burial, which suggests birth around 1753–54 rather than 1760.
  </div>

  <h2>Established core evidence</h2>
  <div class="timeline">
    <article><time>18 Sep 1753</time><h3>William Gally marries Mary Philips</h3><p>Original Lytchett Minster marriage register, <code>PE/LMR:RE 1/3</code>. This is the probable parental marriage for the children baptised to William Galley in 1755, 1758 and 1760.</p></article>
    <article><time>13 Apr 1755</time><h3>John Galley baptised</h3><p>Original register: “John son of Wm Galley was baptized April 13.” Archive reference <code>PE/LMR:RE 1/3</code>.</p></article>
    <article><time>8 Jan 1758</time><h3>William Galley baptised</h3><p>Son of William Galley at Lytchett Minster.</p></article>
    <article><time>20 Apr 1760</time><h3>Mary Galley baptised</h3><p>Daughter of William Galley at Lytchett Minster. This is the leading candidate for Matthew Medway's later wife.</p></article>
    <article><time>13 Aug 1786</time><h3>Matthew Medway marries Mary Galley</h3><p>Poole St James. Matthew is described as a “Sojourner Batchelor”; Mary as “of this Parish Spinster”. This marriage is proved, but the bride's parentage is not named.</p></article>
  </div>

  <h2>Major new evidence: the 1749 Lytchett lease</h2>
  <p>The Dorset History Centre catalogue now identifies the lease that had previously appeared only in a Poole seafarers index. It is highly significant because it explicitly joins the surnames and places that recur later in the Mary Galley case.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Reference</th><th>Date</th><th>Catalogue evidence</th><th>Research significance</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>D-DAS/4434A</code><br/><code>D-DAS/4434B</code></td>
        <td>8 Feb 1749</td>
        <td>Lease/counterpart lease to <strong>William Gally junior, aged 21, of Lytchett Minster, mariner</strong>, for cottage, garden and ground at Lytchett Minster. Mentions <strong>William Gally of Hamworthy</strong>, described as a <strong>“relation”</strong> of William junior, and <strong>Rachael, daughter of Nicholas Minion of Hamworthy, aged 4</strong>.</td>
        <td>Creates a documented Lytchett–Hamworthy Galley/Gally network before Mary 1760 was born. William junior is an excellent candidate for William baptised in 1726 and later husband of Mary Philips.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>D-DAS/4462</code></td>
        <td>7 May 1753</td>
        <td>Counterpart lease of the same Lytchett property to Thomas Rogers, joiner. Mentions Ann Rogers and children, and <strong>William Gally</strong>.</td>
        <td>Potential continuity across the exact year William Gally married Mary Philips. Original wording must be checked before treating this as proof of residence or survival.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>D-DAS/4523</code></td>
        <td>20 Dec 1766</td>
        <td>Later counterpart lease of the Lytchett property to Thomas Rogers; again mentions <strong>William Gally</strong>.</td>
        <td>Potentially preserves the title history of William's former holding. The original is needed to determine whether William is a named life, former tenant or merely recited in the chain of title.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Working interpretation</h3>
  <p>A William Gally baptised at Lytchett Minster on 27 September 1726, son of William, would have been about 22 in February 1749. The lease describes William Gally junior as aged 21. The one-year difference is minor and the chronological fit is strong.</p>
  <p>The suffix “junior” becomes especially important because the same lease names another <strong>William Gally of Hamworthy</strong> as his “relation”. The wording does not prove father and son; “relation” could indicate father, uncle, cousin or another kinship. The original lease is therefore a priority retrieval target.</p>

  <h2>The Minion connection</h2>
  <p>There is an independent earlier marriage that may explain why a young Minion appears in William junior's lease:</p>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>25 March 1744 — Nicholas Minion of Hamworthy × Sarah Gally of Lytchett Minster</h3>
    <p>The 1749 lease names <strong>Rachael, daughter of Nicholas Minion of Hamworthy, aged 4</strong>. If Sarah Gally was a close relative of William junior, Rachael may have been his niece or cousin. That relationship is not yet proved, but the chronology and geography are highly suggestive.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>John Galley as collateral evidence</h2>
  <p>The strongest later support for identifying Mary 1760 with Matthew Medway's wife comes from the probable fate of her elder brother John.</p>
  <div class="timeline">
    <article><time>23 Dec 1797</time><h3>John Galley marries Sarah Hill</h3><p>Original Lytchett Minster register: both are explicitly “of Hamworthy”. John appears to sign his own name; Sarah makes her mark.</p></article>
    <article><time>7 Nov 1798</time><h3>John Galley in Hamworthy militia list</h3><p>John is a <strong>sawyer</strong>, married, apparently with two children, height 5 ft 8 in. The return covers men aged 18–45. A John baptised in April 1755 would be about 43½ and fits the statutory age range almost exactly.</p></article>
    <article><time>7 Nov 1798</time><h3>Matthew Medway in the same return</h3><p>Matthew appears as a labourer with wife and three children. If John is the 1755 son of William, the document potentially places Matthew Medway and his wife's elder brother in the same Hamworthy statutory return.</p></article>
    <article><time>1805 & 1809</time><h3>John and Sarah's daughters</h3><p>Martha Gally was born 2 Oct 1805 and Mary Gally 14 Aug 1809, both of Hamworthy. They were baptised at Poole Congregational on 4 Feb 1813, by which date John was explicitly deceased.</p></article>
  </div>

  <h2>Sarah Hill: leading candidate</h2>
  <p>The strongest candidate for John's wife is <strong>Sarah Hill, baptised at Poole St James on 23 December 1772, daughter of George and Elizabeth Hill</strong>. She would have been about 25 at marriage and in her thirties when Martha and Mary were born.</p>
  <p>This may intersect with Matthew Medway's 1781 court case involving <strong>George Hill and Elizabeth Hill his wife</strong>. That identity has not yet been proved and must not be assumed.</p>

  <h2>Excluded candidate</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <span class="status proved">Excluded</span>
    <h3>Mary Galley baptised 30 Nov 1756, Langton Matravers</h3>
    <p>This Mary can be followed as Mary Cole. A 1801 probate administration commission names <strong>James Galley as her lawful brother and next of kin</strong> and refers explicitly to “your late sister Mary Cole”. This effectively excludes her as the unmarried Mary Galley who married Matthew Medway in 1786.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What is proved, probable and still open?</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Proposition</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>William Gally married Mary Philips at Lytchett Minster in 1753.</td><td><span class="status proved">Proved</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>John 1755, William 1758 and Mary 1760 were children of the same William Gally who married Mary Philips.</td><td><span class="status strong">Highly probable</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>William Gally junior, mariner, in the 1749 lease was William baptised in 1726.</td><td><span class="status strong">Very strong candidate</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>William Gally junior later married Mary Philips in 1753.</td><td><span class="status strong">Strong hypothesis</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>John Galley baptised 1755 became John Galley, sawyer of Hamworthy.</td><td><span class="status strong">Very strong hypothesis</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mary Galley baptised 1760 became Mary Medway.</td><td><span class="status strong">Strong leading candidate</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>William Gally of Hamworthy in the 1749 lease was William junior's father.</td><td><span class="status open">Open — lease says only “relation”</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Archive retrieval priorities</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>D-DAS/4434A/B</strong> — obtain complete scans of lease, counterpart, endorsements, seals and enclosed notes.</li>
    <li><strong>D-DAS/4462</strong> — determine how William Gally is mentioned in the 1753 Rogers lease.</li>
    <li><strong>D-DAS/4523</strong> — determine how William Gally is carried forward in the 1766 lease.</li>
    <li>Search for settlement, apprenticeship, land-tax and poor-law records for <strong>John Galley of Hamworthy</strong>, particularly before 1797.</li>
    <li>Reconstruct <strong>George and Elizabeth Hill</strong> and test whether they are the couple in Matthew Medway's 1781 case.</li>
  </ol>

